{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795149996,"nonce":"92FApk9GD86V29Zizuded79WNyo=","title":"Chart: ship an admission policy that holds step Pods to their own Secrets and ServiceAccount","message":"Decided 2026-11-20 (owner): keep step Pods in \u003crelease ns\u003e-steps by default, and add a cluster-enforced check that works in both modes, including installs that set scarab.namespace to the release namespace.\n\nWhy: any Pod can mount any Secret (secretName, secretKeyRef, envFrom) and use any ServiceAccount in its own namespace. The kubelet resolves these refs without checking the Pod's ServiceAccount, and RBAC cannot restrict by label. Today only Scarab's own code (Pod builder + author_overlay allowlist, 0.5.0) keeps a step Pod from referencing the server's Secret or ServiceAccount. An admission policy makes the cluster refuse it even if Scarab has a bug.\n\nScope:\n- A ValidatingAdmissionPolicy + binding in deploy/helm/scarab (GA in Kubernetes 1.30; check the chart's kubeVersion and say what happens on older clusters: skip with a NOTES warning, or require it).\n- Match: Pod CREATE where request.userInfo is the Scarab server ServiceAccount (the only creator of step Pods), in the step namespace.\n- Refuse: a secret volume, projected secret source, secretKeyRef or envFrom secretRef naming anything but that Pod's own per-Pod Secrets (\u003cpod\u003e-secrets, -registry, -token, -oidc, -workspace, and any other the executor creates — list them from crates/scarab-executor-k8s); serviceAccountName other than the step ServiceAccount or an operator placement-profile ServiceAccount (needs the profile SA names passed in, e.g. a params ConfigMap rendered from scarab.placement); hostPath, hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC; privileged unless the step's governed grant allows it (decide how the policy learns that, or leave privileged to Pod Security).\n- Values: scarab.admissionPolicy.enabled (default true), failurePolicy, validationActions (Deny; Audit for a trial).\n- Plugin Pods and probe Pods (scarab-plugin-k8s) need the same treatment or an explicit exclusion.\n\nTests: scripts/chart_render_check.sh asserts the policy renders in both namespace modes and is absent when disabled. Add a kind case in the cluster suite: a Pod created as the server SA that mounts the server Secret is refused, and a normal step Pod is admitted. The admission-policy denial must surface as a step failure (ExecError::Refused path), not a dead letter.\n\nDocs: deploy-helm.mdx and the chart README (what it enforces, how to run it in Audit first); CHANGELOG Security.","files":null}]}